{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1538374610,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUzNjUzMjE4MjA=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2885","origin":"github"},"title":"`_normalize_paths()` logic is flawed","message":"A directory that actually is a symlink to the root of a dataset gets the `FileNotInRepositoryError` finger\n\n```\nPath outside repository: /tmp/datalad_temp_test_symlinked_relpath_dvazitr/linked\n[Errno None] : Path outside repository: /tmp/datalad_temp_test_symlinked_relpath_dvazitr/linked: '/tmp/datalad_temp_test_syml\n```\n\n(yes, you see that correct: the path in question is identical to the repo base path).\n\nThe reason is the code in _normalize_paths():\n```\npath = opj(realpath(dirname(path)), basename(path))\n```\n\nOnly the `dirname` portion of the path is resolved, but the symlink is the final portion. The backdrop for this failure is:\n\n```\ndef test_symlinked_relpath(path):\n    # initially ran into on OSX https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2406\n    os.makedirs(opj(path, \"origin\"))\n    dspath = opj(path, \"linked\")\n    os.symlink('origin', dspath)\n    ds = Dataset(dspath).create()\n ```\n\nWe don't see this in master ATM, because about a dozen layers before it wrangle the incoming path in a way that shadows the issues.","files":null}]}